1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,280
Doc comments are the one description of a Swift package that sits right beside the code.

2
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:08,140
And most of the time, nobody reads them.

3
00:00:08,890 --> 00:00:09,630
They drift.

4
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:12,750
They have no shape, so each one is different.

5
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:15,030
A tool can't rely on them.

6
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:17,980
gloss starts from a simple bet.

7
00:00:18,380 --> 00:00:21,980
If every doc comment has the same shape, the docs can be checked,

8
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:24,500
and then they can be read as the interface they describe.

9
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:30,580
The linter checks every triple-slash comment against one shape.

10
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:35,450
First, a summary of one or two sentences: what the declaration does,

11
00:00:35,730 --> 00:00:36,390
then why.

12
00:00:37,770 --> 00:00:39,290
Then a blank line.

13
00:00:39,590 --> 00:00:43,810
Then a markup block, with an entry for every parameter, the return value,

14
00:00:43,950 --> 00:00:44,850
and what it throws.

15
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:51,540
Each finding is one line in compiler format, so Xcode and GitHub Actions show it as

16
00:00:51,540 --> 00:00:51,840
it is.

17
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:56,820
The exit status is one when any finding is an error, so the same command can

18
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,060
gate continuous integration.

19
00:01:00,067 --> 00:01:04,167
gloss parses every file once, with swift-syntax.

20
00:01:04,917 --> 00:01:11,117
From that one pass, it builds each declaration's contract, and a dependency graph of types.

21
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:16,247
From those come an interface export, as JSON or a web page.

22
00:01:16,447 --> 00:01:21,047
A map of the package: its modules, folders, and types, and what each uses.

23
00:01:21,347 --> 00:01:25,747
The reason one type depends on another, in the words of the docs gloss already requires.

24
00:01:25,747 --> 00:01:30,847
What changed against a base revision, read straight from git, and what that change reaches.

25
00:01:32,867 --> 00:01:34,667
A branch can explain itself.

26
00:01:34,947 --> 00:01:38,687
It keeps a change story in a file under dot gloss, changes,

27
00:01:38,947 --> 00:01:39,967
named for the branch.

28
00:01:40,717 --> 00:01:43,797
A story has a headline, themes, and notes.

29
00:01:44,317 --> 00:01:46,037
Themes link the types they are about.

30
00:01:46,337 --> 00:01:48,637
A note tells the change from one type's side.

31
00:01:49,387 --> 00:01:51,547
Then gloss story checks it.

32
00:01:51,847 --> 00:01:56,087
It fails while a type whose interface changed has no theme, or no note,

33
00:01:56,267 --> 00:01:58,247
or while a link names nothing.

34
00:01:58,687 --> 00:02:00,767
A change can't land unexplained.

35
00:02:02,733 --> 00:02:07,893
The package is built in layers, and each depends only on the ones below it.

36
00:02:08,543 --> 00:02:10,003
Gloss Read parses the sources.

37
00:02:10,763 --> 00:02:12,483
Gloss Lint holds the house style.

38
00:02:13,243 --> 00:02:15,223
Gloss Model holds what the docs say.

39
00:02:16,123 --> 00:02:18,103
Gloss Map arranges the model for reading.

40
00:02:19,003 --> 00:02:23,363
Gloss Page draws it as a web page, and Gloss App draws it in a macOS

41
00:02:23,363 --> 00:02:23,663
window.

42
00:02:24,413 --> 00:02:30,593
Gloss Lint and Gloss Model never read from each other, so the model holds what the

43
00:02:30,593 --> 00:02:33,193
docs say whether or not the lint runs.

44
00:02:33,943 --> 00:02:38,223
The page and the app draw from the same map, so they can't disagree.

45
00:02:38,623 --> 00:02:42,523
One shape for the docs, one pass over the code, and a package you read through

46
00:02:42,523 --> 00:02:43,143
its own words.
